## Modeling Temperature Alerts

A temperature reading generates an alert. The alert's severity determines the message, but the actual temperature value travels inside the enum variant.

### The Alert Enum

```xiom
enum Alert {
  Normal,
  Hot(temp: Int),
  Cold(temp: Int),
  Extreme(temp: Int),
}
```

### Classifying Temperatures

A function reads the Celsius value and decides which alert to fire:

```xiom
fn check_temperature(celsius: Int) -> Alert {
  if celsius >= 40 { return Alert.Extreme(celsius); }
  if celsius >= 30 { return Alert.Hot(celsius); }
  if celsius <= 0 { return Alert.Cold(celsius); }
  return Alert.Normal;
}
```

### Generating Alert Messages

Each alert type produces a different message:

```xiom
fn Alert.message(self) -> Str {
  match self {
    Normal => { return "Temperature is comfortable."; }
    Hot(temp) => { return "Warning: " + temp + "\u00b0C is hot!"; }
    Cold(temp) => { return "Warning: " + temp + "\u00b0C is cold!"; }
    Extreme(temp) => { return "DANGER: " + temp + "\u00b0C is extreme!"; }
  }
}
```

### Testing the Range

```xiom
check_temperature(-5);   // Cold(-5)
check_temperature(22);   // Normal
check_temperature(35);   // Hot(35)
check_temperature(45);   // Extreme(45)
```

### Using the Alert

```xiom
let alert = check_temperature(42);
alert.message();  // "DANGER: 42°C is extreme!"
```

